> Just to amplify what Simon wrote: Just because you have seen a scroll
> of identify, and therefore can identify new scrolls of identify on
> sight, has no connection with whether you know what a spellbook of
> identify looks like.  Since you've never identified a spellbook of
> identify, it is considered unknown to you.

I thought about this for a while, but in the end decided it made
sense. One only needs to be able to read in order to trigger a
scroll's effect - "You pronounce the magic words" - and you can do
this even when you're blind, have your hands glued to a staff, or
polymorphed. But since you need to have your hands free to cast
spells, they obviously also involve a gestural component, which is
presumably written down in the spellbook along with the words you have
to speak.
Since scrolls have no "action" component at all and can be written by
any (literate) character with a magic marker (that is, there's no
special ceremony involved in writing the scroll, just a knowledge of
the proper words will suffice) those words MUST be more complex than
the words spoken by a wizard casting the corresponding spell, since it
has non-verbal components which the scroll can't use.

However, it would be very odd if there was NO relationship between the
two, especially to a skilled wizard who can spot such things. You
might have to say "Abracadabra" and throw a pinch of powdered newt
into the air to trigger spell X, and write "Abracadabra iggledy
gobbledy gook" on a scroll to produce the same effect, but one would
expect some duplication. I would propose that knowledge of a given
spell should increase a wizard chance of writing the corresponding
scroll to 1/2 (from 1/3) and vice versa.

